 1. Selection Sort

📌 Problem Statement  
Sort an array of integers using the selection sort algorithm.

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

void selection_sort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int mini = i;
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[mini]) {
                mini = j;
            }  
        }
        int temp = arr[mini];
        arr[mini] = arr[i];
        arr[i] = temp;
    }

    cout << "After selection sort: " << "\n";
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << "\n";
}


---
   
🧠 Approach  
Selection Sort works by repeatedly selecting the smallest element from the unsorted part and placing it at the correct position.

Steps:
1. Start from the first index  
2. Find the minimum element in the remaining array  
3. Swap it with the current index  
4. Repeat until the array is sorted  

---

⚙️ Implementation Details  
- Maintain an index for the minimum element  
- Compare elements in the unsorted portion  
- Swap the smallest element with the current position  

---

⏱ Time Complexity  
O(N²)

💾 Space Complexity  
O(1)

---

🎯 Key Concepts  
- In-place sorting  
- Comparison-based algorithm  
- Simple but inefficient for large inputs  

---

✅ Outcome  
Sorts the array by repeatedly placing the smallest element in its correct position.





2. Bubble Sort

📌 Problem Statement  
Sort an array of integers using the bubble sort algorithm.

class BubbleSort {
public:
    void bubble_sort(vector<int>& arr) {
        int n = arr.size();
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int didSwap = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j <= i - 1; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                    swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
                    didSwap = 1;
                }
            }
            if (didSwap == 0) {
                break;
            }
        }

        cout << "After Using Bubble Sort:\n";
        for (int num : arr) {
            cout << num << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }
};


---

🧠 Approach  
Bubble Sort works by repeatedly comparing adjacent elements and swapping them if they are in the wrong order.

Steps:
1. Traverse the array multiple times  
2. Compare adjacent elements  
3. Swap them if they are not in correct order  
4. After each pass, the largest element moves to its correct position  
5. Stop early if no swaps are performed  

---

⚙️ Implementation Details  
- Use nested loops to compare adjacent elements  
- Track swaps using a flag (didSwap)  
- If no swaps occur in a pass, the array is already sorted  

---

⏱ Time Complexity  
O(N²) (Worst and Average)  
O(N) (Best case when already sorted)

💾 Space Complexity  
O(1)

---

🎯 Key Concepts  
- Adjacent swapping  
- Optimization using swap flag  
- Stable sorting algorithm  

---

✅ Outcome  
Sorts the array by repeatedly pushing the largest element to the end in each iteration.





3. Insertion Sort

📌 Problem Statement  
Sort an array of integers using the insertion sort algorithm.

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> insertionSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            int key = nums[i];
            int j = i - 1;

            while (j >= 0 && nums[j] > key) {
                nums[j + 1] = nums[j];
                j--;
            }

            nums[j + 1] = key;
        }
        return nums;
    }
};


---

🧠 Approach  
Insertion Sort builds the sorted array one element at a time by inserting each element into its correct position.

Steps:
1. Start from the second element  
2. Compare it with previous elements  
3. Shift all larger elements one position to the right  
4. Insert the current element at its correct position  
5. Repeat for all elements  

---

⚙️ Implementation Details  
- Treat the first element as already sorted  
- Use a key variable to store current element  
- Shift elements greater than key  
- Insert key at the correct position  

---

⏱ Time Complexity  
O(N²) (Worst and Average)  
O(N) (Best case when already sorted)

💾 Space Complexity  
O(1)

---

🎯 Key Concepts  
- In-place sorting  
- Incremental building of sorted array  
- Stable sorting algorithm  

---

✅ Outcome  
Efficient for small datasets and nearly sorted arrays by placing elements in their correct position step by step.





4. Merge Sort

📌 Problem Statement  
Sort an array of integers using the merge sort algorithm.

class Solution {
public:
    void merge(vector<int>& arr, int low, int mid, int high) {
        vector<int> temp;
        int left = low, right = mid + 1;

        while (left <= mid && right <= high) {
            if (arr[left] <= arr[right])
                temp.push_back(arr[left++]);
            else
                temp.push_back(arr[right++]);
        }

        while (left <= mid)
            temp.push_back(arr[left++]);

        while (right <= high)
            temp.push_back(arr[right++]);

        for (int i = low; i <= high; i++)
            arr[i] = temp[i - low];
    }

    void mergeSort(vector<int>& arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low >= high)
            return;

        int mid = (low + high) / 2;

        mergeSort(arr, low, mid);
        mergeSort(arr, mid + 1, high);

        merge(arr, low, mid, high);
    }
};


---

🧠 Approach  
Merge Sort follows the divide and conquer strategy by dividing the array into smaller parts, sorting them, and then merging them.

Steps:
1. Divide the array into two halves  
2. Recursively sort both halves  
3. Merge the sorted halves into one sorted array  
4. Repeat until the entire array is sorted  

---

⚙️ Implementation Details  
- Recursively split the array using mid index  
- Use a temporary array to merge sorted halves  
- Compare elements from both halves and insert in sorted order  
- Copy merged elements back to the original array  

---

⏱ Time Complexity  
O(N log N)

💾 Space Complexity  
O(N)

---

🎯 Key Concepts  
- Divide and conquer  
- Recursion  
- Merging sorted arrays  

---

✅ Outcome  
Efficiently sorts the array in O(N log N) time, making it suitable for large datasets.





5. Recursive Bubble Sort

📌 Problem Statement  
Sort an array of integers using the recursive version of the bubble sort algorithm.

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

void bubble_sort(int arr[], int n) {
    if (n == 1) return;

    int didSwap = 0;

    for (int j = 0; j <= n - 2; j++) {
        if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
            int temp = arr[j + 1];
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
            arr[j] = temp;
            didSwap = 1;
        }
    }

    if (didSwap == 0) return;

    bubble_sort(arr, n - 1);
}



---

🧠 Approach  
This solution applies bubble sort recursively by performing one pass and then solving the smaller subproblem.

Steps:
1. Perform one pass to move the largest element to the end  
2. Check if any swap occurred  
3. If no swaps, the array is already sorted  
4. Recursively call the function for the remaining array of size n-1  

---

⚙️ Implementation Details  
- Base case: if array size is 1, return  
- Use a flag to track swaps  
- Perform one full pass in each recursive call  
- Reduce problem size by one after each call  

---

⏱ Time Complexity  
O(N²)

💾 Space Complexity  
O(N) (due to recursion stack)

---

🎯 Key Concepts  
- Recursion  
- Bubble sort logic  
- Optimization using swap flag  

---

✅ Outcome  
Sorts the array recursively while reducing unnecessary passes using early exit optimization.





6. Recursive Insertion Sort

📌 Problem Statement  
Sort an array of integers using the recursive version of the insertion sort algorithm.

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

void insertion_sort(int arr[], int i, int n) {
    if (i == n) return;

    int j = i;
    while (j > 0 && arr[j - 1] > arr[j]) {
        int temp = arr[j - 1];
        arr[j - 1] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
        j--;
    }

    insertion_sort(arr, i + 1, n);
}

---

🧠 Approach  
This solution builds the sorted array recursively by inserting each element into its correct position.

Steps:
1. Assume the first element is already sorted  
2. Take the next element and compare it with previous elements  
3. Shift larger elements to the right  
4. Place the current element at its correct position  
5. Recursively process the next element  

---

⚙️ Implementation Details  
- Base case: when index reaches array size  
- Use a loop to shift elements greater than current value  
- Swap elements until correct position is found  
- Recursively move to the next index  

---

⏱ Time Complexity  
O(N²)

💾 Space Complexity  
O(N) (due to recursion stack)

---

🎯 Key Concepts  
- Recursion  
- Incremental sorting  
- In-place element shifting  

---

✅ Outcome  
Sorts the array recursively by inserting each element into its correct position step by step.





7. Quick Sort

📌 Problem Statement  
Sort an array of integers using the quick sort algorithm.

---

🧠 Approach  
Quick Sort uses the divide and conquer strategy by selecting a pivot element and partitioning the array around it.

Steps:
1. Choose a pivot element (here, last element)  
2. Rearrange the array so that:
   - elements smaller than pivot come before it  
   - elements greater than pivot come after it  
3. Place pivot at its correct position  
4. Recursively apply the same process to left and right subarrays  

---

⚙️ Implementation Details  
- Use partition function to place pivot correctly  
- Maintain an index to track smaller elements  
- Swap elements to ensure correct ordering  
- Recursively sort left and right partitions  

---

⏱ Time Complexity  
O(N log N) (Average case)  
O(N²) (Worst case)

💾 Space Complexity  
O(log N) (due to recursion stack)

---

🎯 Key Concepts  
- Divide and conquer  
- Partitioning logic  
- Recursion  

---

✅ Outcome  
Efficiently sorts the array by recursively partitioning around a pivot element.
